WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.650
Your grandfather says, "In our day, people were more honest." Meanwhile you carry in your pocket a world he could never have imagined.

2
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:15.140
Which of you is right: is humanity really moving forward, or is it only speeding up and mistaking speed for progress?

3
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.710
Picture a family table.

4
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:34.640
It's a holiday afternoon, the tea has steeped, and Grandpa leans back and begins: In our day, people greeted one another.

5
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.970
You never walked past a neighbor's door without knocking.

6
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:42.740
Bread tasted like bread, and tomatoes smelled like tomatoes.

7
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:54.230
At the other end of the table, a grandchild is looking at their phone, perhaps at that very moment texting a friend: Oh, we had the cartoons of our childhood, and nothing today even comes close.

8
00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:59.320
Two generations at the same table, two separate golden ages.

9
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.130
One looks back sixty years, the other fifteen.

10
00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:07.740
And both believe it sincerely: things used to be better.

11
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.430
This feeling is not our invention.

12
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:18.880
Some two thousand seven hundred years ago, the Greek poet Hesiod told the history of humankind through metals in his long poem, Works and Days.

13
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:27.170
First came the golden race: people who lived like gods, who never tired, never aged, and met death as though drifting off to sleep.

14
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.780
Then came the silver race, then the bronze, then the heroes.

15
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.550
And last of all, the iron race, the poet's own generation.

16
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:43.560
Hesiod laments that he wishes he had not been born into this age, that he had either died before it or been born after.

17
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.890
Weariness by day, sorrow by night.

18
00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.820
Children are nothing like their fathers, and guests show no respect to their hosts.

19
00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.670
Sound familiar?

20
00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:59.760
A few centuries later, the Roman poet Horace descends the same staircase.

21
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:10.130
In one of his odes he writes: Our fathers' age, worse than our grandfathers', bore us, more wicked still, and we shall soon bring forth a generation even more depraved.

22
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:18.260
Just think: if every generation since Horace had been worse than the one before, what kind of place would we be living in today?

23
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.470
The math doesn't add up.

24
00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:25.560
Which means this complaint holds something that says more about people than about the world.

25
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.690
Interestingly, this feeling was once given a name as an illness.

26
00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:38.940
In sixteen eighty-eight, Johannes Hofer, completing his medical studies in Basel, examined a strange affliction among Swiss mercenaries fighting far from home.

27
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:45.630
The soldiers lost their appetite, lost their sleep, and sometimes wasted away to the point of mortal danger.

28
00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:51.360
Hofer named the condition by combining the Greek words for homecoming and pain: nostalgia.

29
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.010
So nostalgia began as a longing for a place.

30
00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.300
Over time the place faded away, and what remained was time.

31
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.950
Now we long not for our village, but for our youth.

32
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.120
So why does the past always seem brighter?

33
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.090
Psychologists attribute part of it to the way memory works.

34
00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:15.660
Over time, the mind files down painful details and polishes the good moments.

35
00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:21.870
Someone recalling their military service remembers not the cold on night watch, but the laughter in the barracks.

36
00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:29.040
And there is another reason: most of us remember the old days alongside ages when we carried no responsibility.

37
00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.130
Perhaps that tomato smelled better simply because we were ten years old when we smelled it.

38
00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:42.260
We weren't the ones paying the bills or worrying about illness; the world seemed flawless to us because keeping it flawless wasn't our job.

39
00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.110
But we need to be careful here.

40
00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:49.560
Dismissing every nostalgia as an illusion would be the easy way out.

41
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.410
Some things really are lost.

42
00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:59.740
A neighborhood is torn down, a language is forgotten, a craft is buried along with its last master.

43
00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:04.190
Grandpa's point about greetings may not be entirely imaginary either.

44
00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:14.720
The real question is this: when the losses and gains are tallied up, is the story of humanity climbing upward, sliding downward, or tracing a circle and turning in place?

45
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.650
This is precisely the question around which the philosophy of progress is born.

46
00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:25.220
And that question takes on its sharpest form at a moment when a human life is up for bargaining.

47
00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.870
Paris, autumn of seventeen ninety-three.

48
00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:32.360
The Revolution has begun to devour its own children.

49
00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.530
The guillotine is at work almost every day.

50
00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:41.420
On the Rue Servandoni, in the house of a widow named Madame Vernet, a man is hiding.

51
00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:44.430
His name is Nicolas de Condorcet.

52
00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:53.200
A mathematician, permanent secretary of the Academy of Sciences, an opponent of slavery, and one of the rare voices defending women's right to vote.

53
00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:02.450
He had applauded the Revolution and taken a seat in the assembly, but because he criticized the Jacobins' draft constitution, a warrant had been issued for his arrest.

54
00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:06.100
Every knock at the door must have made his heart leap into his throat.

55
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:11.670
What would you expect such a man to write, in days when death waited just one street away?

56
00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:18.200
A last will, perhaps a cry of rage, perhaps a bitter reckoning with how dark humanity can be.

57
00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.050
Condorcet does none of these things.

58
00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:26.620
He sits down and writes his Sketch for a Historical Picture of the Progress of the Human Mind.

59
00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.950
He divides human history into ten epochs.

60
00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:38.720
He begins with hunter-gatherer tribes and moves on to agriculture, writing, Greek philosophy, the printing press, and the scientific revolution.

61
00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.330
The tenth epoch, however, is the one not yet lived: the future.

62
00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:49.380
And that future, for a man looking out from the room where he lay in hiding, is astonishingly bright.

63
00:05:49.380 --> 00:06:01.030
Condorcet foresees that inequality between nations will diminish, that inequality within each nation will recede as well, and that humanity can truly perfect itself, both in reason and in morals.

64
00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:07.560
He writes that the inequality of rights between women and men will be abolished, that education will reach everyone,

65
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:14.570
and that with the advance of medicine the human lifespan will stretch so far that no one will be able to fix its limit with certainty.

66
00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:21.260
In his view, there is no end to humanity's perfection; only its pace changes, never its direction.

67
00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:28.430
In March of seventeen ninety-four, Condorcet leaves his hiding place so as not to endanger the woman who had sheltered him.

68
00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:33.200
South of Paris, worn out by hunger and fatigue, he enters an inn.

69
00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:41.490
He is in disguise, but his hands are not the hands of a peasant, and, so the story goes, he carries a volume of Horace in his pocket.

70
00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:45.940
Arrested on suspicion, he is locked in a cell at Bourg-la-Reine.

71
00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.590
Two days later, he is found dead in that cell.

72
00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.960
Poison or exhaustion?

73
00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.290
Even today, no one knows for certain.

74
00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:59.580
His book is published after his death, in seventeen ninety-five.

75
00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:08.350
Perhaps the most optimistic text ever written about the progress of humanity came from the pen of a man who fell victim to a revolution founded in the name of progress.

76
00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:11.840
That irony sits at the very heart of our subject.

77
00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:19.170
Yet some half a century before Condorcet, another French-speaking thinker had dealt this same faith its first serious blow.

78
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:27.460
In the summer of seventeen forty-nine, Jean-Jacques Rousseau of Geneva was walking to Vincennes to visit his friend Diderot, who was imprisoned there,

79
00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:33.510
when he read in the journal he carried an announcement of a prize competition held by the Academy of Dijon.

80
00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:40.840
The question was this: Has the restoration of the sciences and the arts contributed to the purification of morals?

81
00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:46.890
Rousseau would later say that the moment he read those lines, he saw another world and became another man.

82
00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:50.060
He says he collapsed at the foot of a tree and wept.

83
00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:54.350
Rousseau's answer defies all the optimism of his age: No.

84
00:07:54.350 --> 00:08:01.520
In his view, the sciences and the arts had done nothing but weave garlands of flowers over the chains around humanity's neck.

85
00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:10.450
As society grew more refined, people lost their sincerity, learned to appear other than they were, and surrendered themselves to display and comparison.

86
00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:16.180
With this objection, Rousseau won the prize in seventeen fifty and became famous overnight.

87
00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:24.310
A few years later, in his second great essay, which questions the origins of inequality, he deepens this idea further still.

88
00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:29.400
And here he introduces a very subtle concept: perfectibility.

89
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:35.610
What sets humans apart from animals is the capacity to change themselves, to improve themselves.

90
00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:39.740
But Rousseau does not present this capacity as something to praise.

91
00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:48.190
He says that this very same capacity is what tore humanity away from the peace of its natural state and drove it toward endless desires and envies.

92
00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:53.280
If perfectibility is a ladder, that ladder reaches just as far down as it does up.

93
00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:59.970
When we set these two men side by side, the skeleton of a debate from our own everyday lives emerges before us.

94
00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:09.700
Today, with the phone in our pocket, we can reach all the libraries of the world; the spread of knowledge that Condorcet dreamed of has perhaps come true in the most striking way.

95
00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:20.070
But with that same phone, we also live out every day the very thing Rousseau feared: polishing our image in the eyes of others, constantly weighing ourselves against everyone else.

96
00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.760
Which of them was right?

97
00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:27.210
Perhaps the point is that both saw two faces of the same instrument.

98
00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:34.060
Knowledge advances; but the heart of the person who uses it is under no obligation to advance at the same pace.

99
00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:39.630
Within a hundred years, this quarrel in Europe had moved to an entirely different part of the world.

100
00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:46.960
This time, the question was not simply whether progress was good or bad; it was the question of whose progress it was.

101
00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:53.810
In the middle of the nineteenth century, a new word gains weight in the language of Ottoman intellectuals: terakki.

102
00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:57.140
Progress, ascent, development.

103
00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:03.990
With the Tanzimat reforms, this word becomes more than a wish; it turns almost into state policy.

104
00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:10.040
But progress shakes a person when it is seen with one's own eyes, not on paper.

105
00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:15.610
And some of the generation who first saw it with their own eyes saw it along the roads of exile.

106
00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:27.420
Ziya Pasha, one of the leading figures among the intellectuals known as the Young Ottomans, lives in Europe from eighteen sixty-seven to eighteen seventy-two; he sees Paris, London, Geneva.

107
00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:38.270
A couplet he left behind is still recited from memory today: Diyar-ı küfrü gezdim, beldeler, kâşaneler gördüm; dolaştım mülk-i İslam'ı, bütün viraneler gördüm.

108
00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:46.560
That is: I traveled the lands of the unbelievers, and I saw cities and mansions; I wandered the realm of Islam, and everywhere I saw ruins.

109
00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:49.890
These two lines lay open the wound of an era.

110
00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:55.460
Because in the minds of this generation there is another powerful understanding of history as well.

111
00:10:55.460 --> 00:11:03.270
In the Islamic world, the best age was placed not in the future but in the past: the Asr-ı Saadet, the Age of Bliss.

112
00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:10.440
The era in which the Prophet and his close companions lived, when faith and justice were thought to exist in their purest form.

113
00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:16.330
In this view, history resembles a river that grows murkier the farther it flows from its source.

114
00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:21.420
To move toward the good is not to rush forward, but to return to the source.

115
00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:25.230
Now imagine these two ideas colliding in a single mind.

116
00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:35.760
On one side, the streets of London lit by gas lamps, the railways, the factories, the libraries; on the other, the belief that the finest age lay thirteen centuries in the past.

117
00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.730
Ziya Pasha's couplet is the cry of exactly this predicament.

118
00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:44.500
If the best is behind us, then what is this progress before my eyes?

119
00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:47.830
And if this is progress, why is it not in my world?

120
00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:52.600
Namık Kemal does not turn away from this question; he walks straight into it.

121
00:11:52.600 --> 00:12:02.330
In eighteen seventy-two, in an article titled Terakki, or Progress, published in the newspaper İbret, he all but takes his reader by the hand and leads them through London.

122
00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:12.380
He describes the schools, the exhibitions, the gardens, and the eagerness with which people pursue knowledge and art, with an enthusiasm that is almost the stuff of fairy tales.

123
00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:16.030
But Namık Kemal's purpose is not to admire Europe.

124
00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:24.960
He wants to show that this progress belongs to no single race or religion, that it is something any society can reach through labor, education, and liberty.

125
00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:34.850
He even goes a step further, arguing that nothing in the essence of Islam stands in the way of science and reason, and that decline springs not from faith but from governance and neglect.

126
00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:45.380
At this point, we need to recall a far older voice from Eastern thought, because the question of history's direction had been pondered deeply in these lands centuries before the Ottoman intellectuals.

127
00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:55.110
In the fourteenth century, the North African thinker Ibn Khaldun examined the rise and fall of societies in the Muqaddimah, which he completed in thirteen seventy-seven.

128
00:12:55.110 --> 00:13:02.440
In his eyes, history neither climbs upward in a straight line nor sinks steadily downward; it traces a circle.

129
00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:10.250
Communities living in the harsh conditions of the desert are tightly bound to one another, and Ibn Khaldun calls this spirit of solidarity asabiyyah.

130
00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:13.900
With this strength, they conquer cities and found states.

131
00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:18.990
But the comfort, luxury, and ease of city life slowly loosen that bond.

132
00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:30.320
According to Ibn Khaldun, a dynasty usually completes this journey in three generations, roughly a hundred and twenty years: the generation that builds, the generation that sustains,

133
00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:34.290
and the generation that finds everything ready-made and squanders it.

134
00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:41.940
Ibn Khaldun's model whispers something to us: nostalgia and the belief in progress may in fact be two sides of the same coin.

135
00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:47.670
Luxury and comfort, the very fruits of progress, also carry the seeds of decline.

136
00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:55.800
This is surprisingly close to Rousseau's concern; but Ibn Khaldun presents it not as a moral sermon, but as an observable social law.

137
00:13:55.800 --> 00:14:01.370
The way out that the Ottoman intellectuals were searching for was a bridge between these two extremes.

138
00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:12.060
At the start of the twentieth century, Mehmet Akif captured this search in two lines of his poem From the Pulpit of the Süleymaniye: we must draw our inspiration directly from the Quran,

139
00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:15.870
and make Islam speak to the understanding of the age.

140
00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:21.280
In other words, returning to the source and keeping pace with the times are not enemies.

141
00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:27.170
It is possible to draw inspiration from the past and let it speak in a language today can understand.

142
00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:33.700
In Akif's vision, the Age of Bliss becomes not a destination to return to, but a compass.

143
00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:36.710
This debate is not foreign to us.

144
00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:43.880
Even today, as we embrace new technologies and new ways of city life, we also speak of the values we have lost.

145
00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:55.530
That unease, swinging between Ziya Pasha's two lines and Namık Kemal's tour of London, perhaps lives on in someone walking the gleaming corridors of a shopping mall, longing for the sounds of the old bazaar.

146
00:14:55.530 --> 00:15:06.380
But there was something these hopeful or anxious debates of the nineteenth century did not yet know: the most brilliant instruments of progress would very soon be used in the darkest scenes of human history.

147
00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:14.510
In nineteen twenty-one, a young German thinker, Walter Benjamin, buys a small picture by the painter Paul Klee.

148
00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:18.640
Its name is Angelus Novus, the New Angel.

149
00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:27.890
Drawn on paper with just a few lines, it shows a strange being, its eyes wide open in astonishment, its mouth agape, its wings spread.

150
00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:30.900
Benjamin carries this picture with him for years.

151
00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.870
In every home he moves into, he hangs it on the wall.

152
00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:44.760
In nineteen forty, as Europe burns once again, Benjamin, of Jewish descent and fleeing the Nazis, writes one of his last texts: On the Concept of History.

153
00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:51.610
In the ninth section of this work, made up of short, numbered reflections, he returns to the New Angel.

154
00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:55.580
For Benjamin, this is the angel of history.

155
00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:58.430
Its face is turned toward the past.

156
00:15:58.430 --> 00:16:06.560
Where we perceive a chain of events, it sees one single catastrophe, which keeps piling wreckage upon wreckage and hurling it at its feet.

157
00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:12.770
The angel would like to stay, to awaken the dead, and to make whole what has been smashed.

158
00:16:12.770 --> 00:16:19.780
But a storm is blowing from Paradise; it has caught in the angel's wings with such force that the angel can no longer close them.

159
00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:27.750
The storm irresistibly propels it into the future, to which its back is turned, while the pile of debris before it grows skyward.

160
00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:33.160
And Benjamin writes the final sentence: this storm is what we call progress.

161
00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:44.170
A few months after writing these lines, in September nineteen forty, in the small town of Portbou on the Spanish border, Benjamin takes his own life, fearing he would be turned back.

162
00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:52.940
Roughly a hundred and fifty years separate Condorcet's hopeful vision from Benjamin's angel; both men wrote as fugitives, both wrote in the shadows.

163
00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:58.030
One looked to the future and saw light, the other looked to the past and saw ruins.

164
00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:00.560
So where are we at the end of this journey?

165
00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:03.890
Was everything really better in the old days?

166
00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:07.860
Some questions are easier to answer with numbers.

167
00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:16.470
By historical estimates, around eighteen hundred, more than forty percent of the children born in the world did not live to see their fifth birthday.

168
00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:19.480
Today that figure is around four percent.

169
00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:24.410
For a moment, think of this not as an abstract statistic, but as a home.

170
00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:29.020
Most of our great-great-grandmothers had buried at least one of the children they bore.

171
00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:34.750
That loss was a silence in those homes, never spoken of, yet known to everyone.

172
00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:40.800
In that old neighborhood Grandfather longs for, perhaps one house in every three held such a silence.

173
00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:48.610
Literacy, vaccines, the wiping of smallpox from the face of the earth: these show how much of Condorcet's dream came true.

174
00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:51.460
But Benjamin's angel is right as well.

175
00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:58.790
The same century turned chemistry to the service of death camps, and physics to bombs that could erase whole cities in an instant.

176
00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:07.560
Knowledge advanced and tools grew ever more refined, but the question of who would use them, and to what end, was never solved in any laboratory.

177
00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:15.850
Rousseau's warning was borne out, two centuries later, in the harshest way imaginable: perfectibility could carry humanity in either direction.

178
00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:19.820
Perhaps the question itself is framed the wrong way.

179
00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:28.910
When we ask whether everything was better in the old days, we act as if there were a single set of scales, yet the pans of those scales hold very different things.

180
00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:34.480
In some areas, such as health, knowledge and lifespan, progress is real and measurable.

181
00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:40.850
In others, such as meaning, solidarity and peace of mind, the picture is more complicated.

182
00:18:40.850 --> 00:18:51.380
As Ibn Khaldun warned, comfort can loosen the bonds between people; and as Akif suggested, the past may be not a place to return to, but a compass that helps us find our way.

183
00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:58.070
Perhaps the most honest conclusion we can reach today is this: we are not better, we are more aware.

184
00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:06.040
Hesiod waited for the end of the race of iron; we know what that race of iron is, how it works, and at what cost we have arrived where we are.

185
00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:12.730
Our grandmothers did not know why their children died; we do, and that knowledge carries a responsibility.

186
00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:20.860
Ziya Pasha saw the ruins, yet was content merely to debate their cause; we have the entire history of that debate laid out before us.

187
00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:24.190
This awareness gives nostalgia a new meaning, too.

188
00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:32.800
When Grandfather, at the holiday table, says that in his day people greeted one another, there is no need to pull out statistics in order to doubt him.

189
00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:38.050
We can hear his words not as a claim about history, but as a statement of values.

190
00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:42.660
What he is really saying is this: this matters, do not lose it.

191
00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:47.750
Nostalgia is not proof that the past was better; it is a map of what we hold dear.

192
00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:55.560
The cartoons the grandchild misses, and the greetings the grandfather misses, both join the list of things we do not want to lose.

193
00:19:55.560 --> 00:20:01.450
Benjamin's angel looked backward because it did not trust a progress that ignored the wreckage.

194
00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:09.580
But having its face turned toward the past did not mean it wished to return there; it wanted to awaken the dead and make whole what had been smashed.

195
00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:17.390
Perhaps that is what we can do as well: we cannot stop the storm, but with our wings spread, we can choose not to close our eyes.

196
00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:23.760
We can learn to look at the past with both gratitude and criticism, and to both celebrate progress and question it.

197
00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:32.850
In that small room, Condorcet chose to face the future with confidence; Rousseau, weeping at the foot of a tree, reckoned the price of civilization.

198
00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:39.220
Ibn Khaldun saw a circle, Namık Kemal a rising road, Benjamin a storm.

199
00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:42.390
None of them alone shows the whole picture.

200
00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:52.440
But someone who listens to all of them together can lean in, with a little more tenderness and a little more alert an ear, both to a grandfather's story and to a grandchild's complaint.

201
00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:55.610
Perhaps the golden age never existed.

202
00:20:55.610 --> 00:21:01.180
But longing for it reminds us of what we must protect, and of what we have yet to build.

203
00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:05.150
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Felsefe.

204
00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:08.000
Until we meet again in a new story.
